              "text": "Depending on the context, **Catherine McCoy** (or its common spelling variation **Katherine McCoy**) can refer to several different notable real-life people, historical figures, or fictional characters:\n\n### 1. Katherine McCoy (American Graphic Designer & Educator)\nIf you are looking for a prominent figure in the professional and academic world, you are likely thinking of **Katherine McCoy** (born October 12, 1945). \n* **Who she is:** She is a highly influential American graphic designer, theorist, and educator. \n* **What she is known for:** Alongside her husband, Michael McCoy, she co-chaired the graduate Design Department at the prestigious **Cranbrook Academy of Art** from 1971 to 1995. Under their leadership, Cranbrook became a legendary hub for experimental, postmodern, and deconstructivist design. She is a past president of the Industrial Designers Society of America (IDSA) and was awarded the AIGA Medal in 1994 for her contributions to design education and practice.\n\n### 2. Catherine McCoy (Fictional Character in *The Great Gatsby* Retellings)\nIn literary circles, Catherine McCoy is a character tied to classic American fiction.\n* **Who she is:** In F. Scott Fitzgerald's 1925 novel ***The Great Gatsby***, there is a minor character named **Catherine** who is the sister of Tom Buchanan's mistress, Myrtle Wilson. \n* **The name \"McCoy\":** While Fitzgerald did not give her a last name in the original text, author Jillian Cantor gave her a full backstory and the surname \"McCoy\" in her 2022 feminist retelling of the story, titled ***Beautiful Little Fools***. In this adaptation, Catherine McCoy is portrayed as an independent suffragette living in New York City who is determined to rescue her sister from a toxic marriage.\n\n### 3. Catherine McCoy (Pitcairn Island / *Bounty* History)\nIn historical genealogy, Catherine McCoy is an important figure in the history of the South Pacific.\n* **Who she is:** She was the daughter of **William McCoy**, one of the historic mutineers of the British ship *HMAV Bounty* who fled to Pitcairn Island in 1790. \n* **Her legacy:** She was born on Pitcairn Island and married Arthur Quintal Sr., another descendant of the mutineers. Her son, Arthur Quintal II, eventually became a prominent leader and Magistrate of Pitcairn Island and later Norfolk Island.\n\n### 4. Dr. Kathleen \"Kathy\" McCoy (Academics & Authors)\nThere are also a few notable academic figures who go by this name:\n* **Dr. Kathy McCoy (Immunologist):** A professor of Physiology and Pharmacology at the University of Calgary and the Scientific Director of the International Microbiome Center, widely recognized for her research on the relationship between the gut microbiome and the immune system.\n* **Dr. Kathleen McCoy (Computer Scientist):** A professor and former chair of the Computer & Information Sciences department at the University of Delaware, specializing in computational linguistics and assistive technologies.\n* **Dr. Kathy McCoy (Psychotherapist/Author):** An American psychotherapist and award-winning author of several books on family relationships and teenage psychology (such as *Coping with Teenage Depression*).",
